      <h1>Revenue Attribution Explained: Connect Traffic to Sales</h1>
      <div class="article-author">By Dzulfikar Ats Tsauri</div>
    </div>

    <div class="article-content">
      <p>You check your analytics every morning. Sessions, pageviews, bounce rate — the numbers move, and some weeks they look great. Then you sit down to plan next month's budget and the dashboard goes quiet. It counted the traffic. It can't tell you which channel earned the money.</p>

      <h2>What Is Revenue Attribution?</h2>
      <p>Revenue attribution connects each sale back to the marketing touchpoints that produced it. Instead of asking how many people visited, it asks which visits turned into revenue, and where they came from.</p>
      <p>Picture a typical path. A founder searches Google, lands on one of your blog posts, reads it, and leaves. Two weeks later she clicks a link in your newsletter, reads a comparison page, and starts a trial. A week after that she upgrades to a paid plan. Who made the sale — Google, the newsletter, or both of them working together? Attribution exists to answer that question, and the answer decides where your next marketing budget goes.</p>

      <h2>Why Sessions and Bounce Rate Don't Answer "Which Channel Makes Money"</h2>
      <p>Standard web analytics were built to count traffic, and they do that well: sessions, pageviews, referrers, devices, locations. What they don't do by default is follow a visitor across visits until money changes hands.</p>
      <p>Sessions are disconnected from each other. Without a way to link visits, the person who discovers you through a blog post and buys through a newsletter link counts as two unrelated visitors. The blog collects a view; the newsletter collects a conversion. The channel that started the journey gets nothing.</p>
      <p>Attention metrics reward the wrong things. Bounce rate treats a reader who finds her full answer in one post and leaves as a failure — yet that post may be the reason she comes back and buys later.</p>
      <p>Direct traffic hides the story. When most of your conversions land under "direct", that usually means someone typed your URL from memory or opened a saved link after discovering you somewhere else. The dashboard credits the last step and calls it the whole journey.</p>

      <h2>Last-Touch vs Multi-Touch, in Plain Words</h2>
      <p>An attribution model is simply the rule for handing out credit for a sale. Two families cover most of the field.</p>
      <p>Last-touch attribution gives all the credit to the final touch before the purchase. It is easy to implement, easy to verify, and it matches how most dashboards report. Its blind spot: it over-rewards channels that close deals and ignores channels that open them. Branded search, retargeting, and direct visits look brilliant, while the blog post that introduced you to the customer earns nothing.</p>
      <p>Multi-touch attribution spreads the credit along the journey. The first touch earns recognition for discovery, middle touches for keeping the visitor engaged, the last for closing. It gives a fairer picture of how channels cooperate, at the price of judgment calls about weighting.</p>
      <p>Neither model is the truth; both are lenses. Pick one, keep it consistent, and compare channels under the same rules. Expect surprises when you switch: the channel that quietly starts most of your journeys almost always deserves more budget than last-touch gives it.</p>

      <h2>Building a Revenue-First Analytics Stack</h2>
      <p>You don't need a data warehouse team to attribute revenue. A small SaaS or online business needs four building blocks:</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>A journey that survives across visits.</strong> Pages, campaigns, and referrers need to connect into one path per visitor, so the journey — not the isolated session — becomes the thing you analyze.</li>
        <li><strong>Conversion events tied to money.</strong> Define the moments that matter: trial signup, demo request, purchase. Track each as an event so every journey ends in a conversion or a visible drop-off.</li>
        <li><strong>Clean traffic sources.</strong> UTM parameters on your own campaigns, referrer data on everything else. Mislabel a source and every model downstream inherits the error.</li>
        <li><strong>A funnel view.</strong> See where journeys stall between first visit and purchase, because a funnel that leaks at step two needs a different fix than one that leaks at the finish.</li>
      </ol>
