      <h2>FAQ</h2>
      <h3>What is revenue attribution in simple terms?</h3>
      <p>It is the practice of tying each sale back to the channel and touchpoints that produced it, so you compare channels by the revenue they bring rather than the traffic they send.</p>
      <h3>Is last-touch attribution wrong?</h3>
      <p>No. It reliably tells you which channel closed the sale, which matters when you are fixing checkout or pricing pages. It just should not be your only lens, because it hides the channels that start journeys, such as search and content.</p>
      <h3>Do I need cookies to track revenue attribution?</h3>
      <p>No. First-party event tracking can link a visitor's page views and actions into one journey without cookies. RevKlik, for example, runs cookieless and stores no personal data, so your site needs no consent banner.</p>

